---
// NDIS cleaning page, built from content-plans/ndis-cleaning.md.
//
// THIS PAGE IS NOT LIKE ITS SIBLINGS. The others sell cleaning to people
// choosing a supplier. This one addresses people with disability, their
// family carers, and coordinators who hold a duty of care to them — and it
// makes claims about how a government scheme works. Three rules follow:
//   1. Factual claims about NDIS rules are advice, not marketing. A
//      participant may act on them. See §15's compliance note.
//   2. Screening claims are safety claims, not trust badges.
//   3. Nothing here is inferred from the sibling pages. Where the brief was
//      blank it stays blank rather than borrowing household-cleaning copy.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH:
//   - §5's "Worth knowing" source copy contained internal strategy written
//     to TLB ("addressable market", "win the enquiry"). REWRITTEN for the
//     reader — see that section. The rewrite needs sign-off.
//   - Four compliance brackets ship visibly per house convention (TLB's
//     ruling), but must be filled before launch: [insured],
//     [police-checked / worker-screened] ×2, [plan-managed and self-managed].
//     NDIS Worker Screening and a police check are DIFFERENT checks — the
//     bracket needs a decision, not just a value.
//   - §15's non-branded FAQs cite a line-item code, a phone number and
//     cancellation rules from the NDIS Pricing Arrangements, which are
//     REISSUED ANNUALLY. Compliance read + "last reviewed" date required.
//
// ✅ NO TEAM SIZE IS STATED ANYWHERE ON THIS PAGE. This note used to read
// "Team size is NINE throughout, per TLB's ruling (the brief's §11 said
// '[five]' while its own trust bar said 9)". Brand Foundation v2.0 §2.4
// settles it the other way: "Never claim that the team is employed
// full-time. It is not accurate and it has been removed from all page copy."
// The headcount went with it, since the disagreement between "five" and
// "nine" was itself evidence the number was not confirmed. §2.4's guidance
// where a figure is unconfirmed is to "use a process promise instead rather
// than deleting the sentence", which is what the §11 story block now does.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import { completeFootprintLocationGroups, footprintSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import TestimonialCarousel from '../../components/sections/TestimonialCarousel.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own two assets, generated per IMAGE-GUIDELINES; the prompts are
// in src/assets/ndis/IMAGE-PROMPTS.md. They fill the §1 hero and the §3 card 3
// slots that had been shipping as dashed placeholder boxes.
//
// Cards 1 and 2 are REUSED rather than generated, per IMAGE-GUIDELINES §5
// ("reuse before generating; same subject = same file"): each card links to
// the page the photo already belongs to, so the frame means the same thing in
// both places. Card 1 is the homepage's regular-clean photo and card 2 is the
// deep-cleaning page's own.
import heroImg from '../../assets/ndis/ndis-cleaning-cleaner-in-a-lived-in-living-room.jpg';
import cardRegularImg from '../../assets/home/house-cleaning-team-vacuuming-open-plan-living-room.jpg';
import cardDeepCleanImg from '../../assets/home/service-deep-and-one-off-cleans.png';
import cardCoordinatorImg from '../../assets/ndis/ndis-cleaning-cleaner-and-support-coordinator-talking-in-a-home.jpg';
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim (the homepage's — no photo supplied for this page yet).
import definitionBgImg from '../../assets/home/house-cleaning-cleaner-wiping-tall-interior-window.png';
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import type { TextBlockBody } from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { theLine, theLineHeading, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';

// ✅ REWRITTEN against the September 2026 copy-rewrite brief (workbook row 20,
// "NDIS cleaning"). This is the largest mapped cluster in the batch at 1,200/mo
// and the page scored lowest, because most of what is wrong here is not fixable
// with words: 700 of those 1,200 searches sit behind two questions only TLB can
// answer. Both are recorded at the top of this file.
//
// TITLE. The old line, "NDIS Cleaning Services | TLB Cleaning", used 36 of the
// available ~60 characters and did not carry `ndis house cleaning` (200/mo)
// contiguously at all. The new one does, and keeps `ndis cleaning services`
// (the old mapped phrase) in the same breath.
//
// 🔴 NOT THE WORKBOOK'S APPROVED TITLE, which is "NDIS House Cleaning & Window
// Cleaning | TLB Cleaning", and not its approved meta either, which ends
// "Windows and ovens too." Both promise services this page cannot yet stand
// behind, and the brief flags both as blocking:
//
//   - WINDOWS, 400/mo across `ndis window cleaning` and `window cleaning ndis`,
//     captured at zero because the word "window" appeared nowhere in the body
//     copy. §16 below now supplies that section, so the gap in the COPY is
//     closed — but the section itself is conditional on confirmation (see its
//     own note), and a title that promises window cleaning above a page whose
//     window section may yet be pulled is a broken click promise on 400/mo.
//     Put "& Window Cleaning" back into the title the same day §16 is confirmed.
//   - OVENS. The only oven demand in this cluster is registration-flavoured:
//     `ndis approved oven cleaning services` 150/mo and `ndis registered oven
//     cleaning` 150/mo are both searched by people specifically checking for a
//     REGISTERED provider. The house rules mark NDIS registration TBC alongside
//     police checks and insurance, and "none of them appears in any copy until
//     confirmed". Nothing on this page writes toward either term, deliberately.
//     See the blocking note at the top of this file.
//
// The description below is the workbook's approved line with that one blocked
// sentence removed. Everything else about it is better than what was here: it
// opens on the mapped phrase contiguously, it names PLAN MANAGERS — who are the
// repeat referrers on this page — and it closes on the approved CTA, which is
// where §7's button label comes from. 123 characters against the old 153, which
// was over the limit.
const pageMeta = {
  title: 'NDIS House Cleaning Services | TLB Cleaning',
  description:
    'NDIS house cleaning delivered by the same friendly cleaner each visit, with clear reporting for plan managers. Enquire today.',
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/house-cleaning/ndis-cleaning/',
};

const business = {
  name: 'TLB Cleaning',
  url: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/',
  logo: '',
  telephone: '+61 404 742 065',
  email: 'hello@tlbcleaning.com.au',
  address: {
    streetAddress: '',
    addressLocality: '',
    addressRegion: '',
    postalCode: '',
    addressCountry: 'AU',
  },
  sameAs: [],
  // ✅ WIDENED to match §17's town list, which the copy-rewrite brief specifies
  // town for town and which includes the nine Southern Gold Coast towns.
  //
  // The old note here read: "NSW only on this page, unlike its siblings —
  // deliberate per the brief's own meta description and suburb list. The NDIS is
  // administered per-state; confirm it's intentional rather than an omission."
  // The new brief answers it the other way, and the structured data has to agree
  // with the band the reader can see rather than contradict it.
  //
  // ⚠️ WORTH CONFIRMING ANYWAY, and it is now a live question rather than a
  // dormant one: a participant's plan is administered under a national scheme,
  // so there is no state barrier of the kind the old note assumed — but if TLB
  // does not in fact take NDIS work across the border, §17 should go back to
  // `nswLocationGroups` and this string with it.
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers and Tweed NSW, and the Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  description: pageMeta.description,
};

// §1 Hero — drafted in full. Lead is 23 words, inside the 15–25 target.
//
// Hero style matches the homepage, house cleaning and deep cleaning pages:
// `split-single-image`, copy left, image right.
//
// THE ASSET HAS LANDED. This slot shipped as a dashed Placeholder box for
// most of this page's life, and the note that used to sit here said the page
// needed it more than any other, because the H1 is literally "the same
// trusted local faces" and the hero had no faces in it. It has one now.
//
// Two consequences of it being a SCENE PHOTO (family A) rather than the
// cut-out figure the slot was originally sized for:
//   - `imageFit` is now UNSET on the render below, taking Placeholder's
//     `cover` default. There is no transparency to preserve and no figure to
//     stand on the section's edge. This is the call the old note anticipated:
//     "if a plain photograph arrives instead, drop `imageFit`".
//   - `ratio` is 1/1 because the SOURCE is 1024×1024, so a box matching the
//     file crops nothing. Regenerate square if this is ever reshot, or change
//     both together.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED, and on this page that distinction carries
// more weight than anywhere else on the site. The woman in the frame does not
// exist. She is honest as generic service imagery — she is not named, not
// captioned as a team member, and not presented as a participant — which is
// the line IMAGE-GUIDELINES §7 draws. But a page whose entire promise is
// "the same familiar faces" is the page where a real photograph of the real
// nine does the most work, and it is still the right thing to shoot before
// launch. Treat this as a good placeholder, not the finished job.
//
// ⚠️ Still true, and still worth keeping: do NOT fill this or any slot on
// this page from src/assets/hero/participant-and-support-worker.png or its
// siblings. Despite the names they are leftover placeholders from the
// Maple/NDIS template this component library was first built against — not
// TLB's people. Using them would fabricate a workforce on the one page where
// that matters most.
// ✅ H1, EYEBROW, LEAD AND CTA ALL REWRITTEN (copy-rewrite brief §4 to §7).
//
// H1: `ndis house cleaning` (200/mo) is scored present or absent and was absent.
// The region extension fixes a second, smaller problem at the same time — the
// page promised the Tweed in its intro and then listed six Northern Rivers
// towns, so putting the footprint in the H1 makes a claim that §17's band now
// has to honour, and does. The stray double space the live markup carried
// between "faces," and "treating" is gone with the split.
//
// The old H1 is NOT cut. It moves one line down into the new `subheading` slot,
// which is what that prop was added to Hero.astro for.
//
// KICKER: this page had no eyebrow label at all, alone among the thirteen pages
// reviewed in this batch. That was a gap rather than a decision. It gives a
// participant or a coordinator immediate confirmation they are in the right
// place before they read a headline.
//
// LEAD: three separate problems in one sentence, all fixed. The mapped phrase
// was absent, which is the opening-paragraph keyword component. "familiar local
// faces, and a completely stress-free schedule" carried an Oxford comma, which
// BFD §9.2 bans. And "Experience the comfort of a safe environment" is
// marketing voice rather than TLB's, which the house rules describe as warm,
// plain and direct. The replacement says the same three things in words
// somebody would actually use.
//
// CTA: the page was running TWO primary labels that contradicted each other on
// the house rules' own terms — "Get an instant quote" on the hero (which the
// rules reserve for regular cleaning) and "Get a fast quote" in the closing
// block (which they reserve for condition-priced work). Running both told a
// reader the business had not decided how it prices this work. The approved CTA
// settles it and suits the audience better than either: a participant or a
// family carer arranging support for the first time is enquiring, not quoting.
//
// ⚠️ WORKBOOK CHANGE REQUEST: "Enquire today" is correct and safe but generic.
// This page's own closing heading, "Tell us what would help.", is better and
// more in voice. Pasted as approved, and raised.
//
// `quoteCta.href` is still the destination, so there is one form to build.
const hero = {
  kicker: 'NDIS cleaning',
  headingLines: ['NDIS house cleaning across the', 'Northern Rivers and the Tweed'],
  subheading: 'The same trusted local faces, treating your home with genuine care.',
  lead: `NDIS house cleaning across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed, from a team you get to know. The same familiar faces, a schedule that does not change on you, and one local number to ring. ${theLine.instant}`,
  cta: { label: 'Enquire today', href: quoteCta.href },
  image: {
    ratio: '1/1',
    label:
      'A TLB cleaner in an aqua polo and dark teal apron standing with her vacuum in a comfortable, lived-in living room, turning toward the camera with a warm, open expression',
    src: heroImg,
  },
};

// §2 Definition paragraph — the brief supplied NOTHING here (the row held
// only "CTA - Get an instant quote"), but this is the answer-target block
// for the focus keyword and the page had no sentence saying what NDIS
// cleaning is.
//
// ⚠️ DRAFTED, NOT SUPPLIED. Sentence one deliberately reuses the brief's own
// branded-FAQ wording ("cleaning usually sits under Core Supports where it's
// reasonable and necessary in your plan") rather than inventing a new
// characterisation of the scheme — so the claim traces to copy TLB already
// wrote. Sentence two is the standard sibling pattern (what TLB does, where).
// Needs sign-off like any other funding statement on this page.
const definitionBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  "NDIS cleaning is regular household cleaning paid for from a participant's NDIS plan, usually under Core Supports where it is reasonable and necessary.",
  'TLB Cleaning provides NDIS cleaning services across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed.',
];

// §3 Pathway cards — drafted. The source titles each end in a literal "→";
// stripped, because PathwayCards renders its own CTA affordance and the
// arrow would duplicate it.
const pathwayCards = {
  // Not in the brief — placeholder structural copy.
  heading: 'However you are arranging support,',
  headingAccent: 'start here.',
  cards: [
    {
      number: 1,
      image: {
        ratio: '4/3',
        label: 'Two TLB cleaners vacuuming and tidying an open-plan living room during a regular visit',
        src: cardRegularImg,
      },
      title: 'Set up regular support',
      description:
        'Most participants prefer the comfort of a consistent, weekly or fortnightly schedule. You get the same familiar face treating your home with genuine care, every time.',
      cta: { label: 'Regular cleaning', href: '/house-cleaning/' },
    },
    {
      number: 2,
      image: {
        ratio: '4/3',
        label: 'A TLB cleaner working through a room during a deep reset clean',
        src: cardDeepCleanImg,
      },
      title: 'I need a deep reset clean',
      description:
        'Sometimes you just need help getting back on top of things. A deep clean resets the home to a safe, comfortable standard before starting regular service.',
      cta: { label: 'Deep cleaning', href: '/house-cleaning/deep-cleaning/' },
    },
    {
      number: 3,
      // ⚠️ STOPGAP DESTINATION. The brief gives this card no route, and it is
      // the page's highest-value click — a coordinator placing participants
      // is a repeat, multi-client referrer. /contact/ stops it stranding,
      // but what a coordinator actually wants is a referral route: how to
      // send a participant, how invoicing works, who to call. None of that
      // exists yet. See the plan's §3.
      // ⚠️ THE LABEL CHANGED WITH THE PHOTO, deliberately. This slot was
      // briefed as "Support coordinator reviewing a participant plan", and
      // that photo was not generated: IMAGE-GUIDELINES §4 bans lettering in
      // frame, and a photo whose entire subject is a document is a photo of
      // garbled fake text — on this page, garbled fake text on something
      // shaped like an NDIS plan. The conversation is where a placement
      // actually gets arranged, so it carries the same meaning with nothing
      // to misread. §6 requires the label to describe the photo that is
      // there, not the one first hoped for.
      image: {
        ratio: '4/3',
        label:
          'A TLB cleaner and a support coordinator talking in the living room of a home, relaxed and unhurried',
        src: cardCoordinatorImg,
      },
      title: 'I am a Support Coordinator',
      description:
        'We make managing plans easy. We are a reliable, [insured] local business ready to provide consistent, respectful in-home help for your participants.',
      cta: { label: 'Talk to us', href: '/contact/' },
    },
  ],
};

// §5 "Worth knowing" — REWRITTEN. This is the page's most important edit.
//
// The brief's source copy ended: "So the addressable market for a good local
// operator is large and growing, and almost nobody explains this clearly to
// the participant. Whoever explains it first tends to win the enquiry."
//
// That is written TO TLB, not to the reader — competitive strategy notes
// pasted into a customer-facing brief. Publishing it would tell an audience
// of people with disability that they are an addressable market and that the
// page exists to win their enquiry. The heading had the same problem ("The
// biggest misconception is also the biggest opportunity" — TLB's
// opportunity, not the reader's).
//
// The FIRST half is genuinely valuable and very likely the
// highest-converting explanation on the page: most participants don't know
// that plan-managed and self-managed funding can go to an unregistered
// provider, and that belief is the objection standing between them and an
// enquiry. Kept, rewritten as guidance addressed to the reader.
//
// ⚠️ The brief's "roughly 65% of active participants" statistic is CUT. It
// was attributed to "the NDIA's own pricing review" with no name or date, it
// does no work for someone deciding whether to call, and it is exactly the
// kind of number that ages. Reinstate only with a proper citation.
//
// First consumer of the new Callout.astro (tone="note").
const worthKnowing = {
  heading: "You probably don't need a registered provider.",
  body: [
    'Most people assume NDIS cleaning has to come from a registered provider. Only NDIA-managed plans are restricted that way.',
    'If your plan is self-managed or plan-managed, you can choose any provider you like, including us. If you are not sure which kind of plan you have, your plan manager or support coordinator will know, and we are happy to talk it through with them.',
  ],
};

// §10a "Who this is for" — drafted. The brief files this as one P row, but
// it is an intro plus a three-item list; TextBlock's `{ list }` body entry
// handles both in source order. Three short phrases don't need
// ServiceBlocks' full row treatment.
const whoThisIsForBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  "If keeping on top of the house has become harder than it used to be, you're not alone, and you don't have to sort it out by yourself.",
  'We clean for:',
  {
    list: [
      'NDIS participants who want a regular, reliable clean at home',
      'Family carers arranging support for someone they love',
      'Support coordinators and plan managers who need a local provider that turns up, communicates and invoices properly',
    ],
  },
];

// §10b Suburbs — now the shared list from src/data/locations.ts, which every
// "Where we clean" section on the site reads from.
//
// This page had been the site's fourth separate suburb list: twelve towns,
// two of which (Bangalow, Goonellabah) appeared nowhere else. Both are in
// the shared Northern Rivers list now, so that inconsistency is resolved in
// their favour rather than by dropping them.
//
// `nswLocationGroups`, not the full list: this page is NSW-only, matching its
// meta description. Plausibly right for a scheme administered per-state, but
// confirm it is deliberate — if TLB takes NDIS work across the border, swap
// to `locationGroups`.
//
// One stale `isHighlighted: true` flag went with the old list. That property
// no longer exists on TagCloud's Tag interface and astro check does not catch
// it (excess-property checking doesn't fire on a const-inferred array passed
// to a prop), so that pill had been silently rendering un-highlighted.


// §11 "Why people choose TLB" — three differentiators.
//
// ⚠️ The brief files this under a "Services (use icons and short text)"
// boilerplate row, but the content is three numbered differentiators, not a
// service list. Mapped to the content, not the label.
//
// LAYOUT: ContentGrid at columns={2}, with the third cell spanning both.
// The three items are structurally uneven — item 1 is a paragraph plus a
// tagline, items 2 and 3 run three paragraphs each — so a 3-up grid would
// leave one short cell beside two long ones. Two short-ish cells on top and
// the longest full-width underneath absorbs that unevenness.
//
// Icon-less: ServiceIcon.astro's set is abstract and has nothing matching
// reliability, familiarity or local ownership.
const whyChooseBlocks = [
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'We turn up',
    body: [
      "For most households a missed clean is annoying. When you've been waiting all week for help with the bathroom, it's a genuinely bad day, which is why reliability isn't something we advertise, it's the thing the whole business was built on.",
      'On time. Done properly. Every time.',
    ],
    tone: 'muted' as const,
  },
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'The same familiar faces, every visit',
    body: [
      "You shouldn't have to explain your home to a stranger every fortnight.",
      "Wherever we can, you'll have the same cleaner each visit, someone who knows how you like things done and doesn't need to be told twice. If they're unwell or away, we'll let you know beforehand and send someone from our team who already knows your preferences.",
      // ✅ v2.0 §9.4 — was "not a national network routing your call through
      // a head office somewhere else", which characterises a competitor in
      // running text. The positive half of the sentence was always the
      // stronger half; it now stands on its own.
      "We're a local team, and there is one local number to ring. When you ring us, you're talking to someone who lives here.",
    ],
    tone: 'muted' as const,
  },
  {
    type: 'text' as const,
    heading: 'A real local business, and a real name behind it',
    body: [
      // ✅ RESOLVED (Brand Foundation v2.0) — was "TLB was started by Teagan,
      // a local mum who wanted the job done properly. It grew from one person
      // to a team of nine without a dollar spent on advertising".
      //
      // The headcount is gone: §2.4 removes it from all page copy. "A local
      // mum" is gone from the sentence's positioning role: §2.2 allows the
      // founder story on the about page, the team section, social and
      // recruitment, and this is a service page.
      //
      // What survives is what §2.4 marks CONFIRMED — "Founded by Teagan
      // Burke, Alstonville" and "Grown by word of mouth, without advertising"
      // — plus §2.2's instruction on how to name her: credited as the
      // founder, not fronted as the brand. Hence "TLB" and "our team" in the
      // surrounding copy, and her full name stated once.
      'TLB was started by Teagan Burke, an Alstonville local who wanted the job done properly. It grew from one person to a team across the region without a dollar spent on advertising, and every client came from someone who had already been looked after.',
      "Most of our team are local women. If you'd feel more comfortable with a woman in your home, tell us and we'll do our best to arrange it.",
      // ⚠️ Two visible brackets, per TLB's bracket convention. Both are
      // launch-blocking: worker vetting is a safety representation on a page
      // inviting staff into the homes of people with disability. And
      // "police-checked / worker-screened" needs a DECISION — NDIS Worker
      // Screening and a police check are different checks, offered here
      // interchangeably with a slash.
      "We're [insured], [police-checked / worker-screened], and bound by the NDIS Code of Conduct like every provider paid from NDIS funding. Your home is yours. We treat it that way.",
    ],
    span: 2,
    tone: 'muted' as const,
  },
];

// §16 NDIS WINDOW CLEANING — NEW SECTION (copy-rewrite brief §16).
//
// 🔴 CONDITIONAL. DO NOT LAUNCH THIS SECTION until somebody confirms TLB cleans
// windows for NDIS participants. If the answer is no, delete this constant and
// its render below — do not soften it. The page's §0 title has already been
// held back for the same reason and can go the other way the day this is
// confirmed.
//
// WHY IT IS BUILT RATHER THAN LEFT OUT: `ndis window cleaning` (200/mo) and
// `window cleaning ndis` (200/mo) are two of this page's eight mapped
// secondaries and were captured at ZERO, because the word "window" did not
// appear anywhere in the body copy. Meanwhile the approved title and meta both
// promised window cleaning. So the workbook was targeting a service the page
// did not mention, and the approved metadata was promising it to anyone who
// clicked. Unlike the registration question at the top of this file, this one
// is fixable in copy the moment somebody says yes: window cleaning is already
// in the site navigation as a home cleaning service
// (/house-cleaning/window-cleaning/), so the service plausibly exists and only
// the NDIS-specific delivery needs confirming.
//
// ⚠️ IT DOES NOT MENTION OVENS, deliberately. The only oven demand in this
// cluster is `ndis approved oven cleaning services` and `ndis registered oven
// cleaning`, both searched by people specifically checking for a REGISTERED
// provider. Writing toward them would imply a regulatory status TLB has not
// confirmed, to the one audience that is checking for it. See the file header.
//
// PLACEMENT: after §13's trust-building grid rather than before it, because a
// participant reads this page for reassurance first and services second. The
// body opens on the REASON rather than the service, per the house rule that a
// sentence about cleaning is a feature and a sentence about not having to think
// about cleaning is a benefit.
//
// THE HEADING carries `ndis window cleaning` contiguously and extends past
// windows, so the section can absorb the other out-of-reach jobs later without
// needing another heading.
//
// ⚠️ ONE LINE TO CHECK WITH THE REST. "It is hourly support" is consistent with
// non-branded FAQ 3 below, which already says the same thing. If windows are
// quoted differently from the hourly support, that sentence comes out.
const windowCleaningBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Windows are the job most people stop doing first. Not because they stop mattering, but because they mean a ladder, a stretch, or a twist that is no longer worth the risk.',
  'We clean windows inside and out at ground level, along with tracks, sills and flyscreens, which is where the dirt actually sits. It can go on the same visit as your regular clean or be booked on its own a few times a year, whichever suits your plan.',
  'Like the rest of the clean, it is hourly support, so it comes out of the same funding and you set the priorities for the time booked. If you would rather we did the windows one visit and the bathrooms the next, that is your call to make and we will work to it.',
];

// §12 Social proof — bracketed [TBC] briefing slots, NOT real testimonials.
//
// ⚠️ The brief supplied NOTHING here — not even the "ask Teagan for
// testimonials" line every sibling brief had. The two angles below are a
// proposal; confirm before briefing Teagan.
//
// ⚠️ EXTRA CARE REQUIRED WHEN THESE ARE FILLED. A quote from an NDIS
// participant is health-adjacent personal information: consent must be
// explicit and informed, and identifying detail (suburb plus disability,
// say) should be minimised. A carer or coordinator quote may be easier to
// obtain and carries similar weight for a coordinator reader.
// Never invent a participant quote.
const testimonials = [
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC. Ask Teagan for a quote from a participant or their family carer about consistency: the same person arriving, on the day they said. Written consent required, and keep identifying detail to a minimum.]',
    name: '[Name, TBC]',
    role: '[Participant or family carer, TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'TLB NDIS client' },
  },
  {
    quote:
      '[TBC. Ask Teagan for a quote from a support coordinator or plan manager about communication and invoicing. Coordinators are repeat referrers, and this is the quote that reaches them.]',
    name: '[Name, TBC]',
    role: '[Support coordinator or plan manager, TBC]',
    rating: 5,
    avatar: { ratio: '1/1', label: 'Support coordinator who refers to TLB' },
  },
];

// §14 Branded FAQs — all five ship. Two carry visible brackets per TLB's
// convention; both are launch-blocking (see the file header).
//
// The brief's first answer carried a stray "(33 words)" — an editing
// artefact from drafting, stripped here. It is not a placeholder.
const brandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Can I use my NDIS funding to pay for cleaning?',
    answer:
      "Often, yes. Cleaning usually sits under Core Supports where it's reasonable and necessary in your plan. We work with [plan-managed and self-managed] participants, and we're glad to talk it through with your coordinator.",
  },
  {
    // ⚠️ The page's single most important trust question. Of the four
    // brackets, resolve this one first.
    question: 'Who actually comes into my home, will it be the same person each time?',
    answer:
      'The same trusted local team wherever we can, familiar faces, not a different stranger each week. TLB is a real local business, insured and accountable [confirm police checks], with Teagan standing behind every job.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What makes TLB different from other cleaners doing NDIS work?',
    answer:
      "A local team with backup. Big enough that your clean still happens when someone is unwell, and local enough that the person who comes knows you and knows the area.",
  },
  {
    question: "What if I'm not happy with the clean, or I want things done differently?",
    answer:
      "Tell us and we'll make it right, and that's the deal. You choose what gets done, how often and what we leave alone. Change it any time. It's your home, your call.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I get in touch?',
    answer:
      "A simple chat about what you need, when, and whether we're the right fit. No pressure, no obligation. We're happy to speak with your family, coordinator or plan manager too.",
  },
];

// §15 Non-branded FAQs — all five drafted, none bracketed, so they READ
// ready to ship. They are not.
//
// ⚠️ COMPLIANCE REVIEW REQUIRED BEFORE PUBLISHING. These are the strongest
// content in the brief and the most consequential, because a participant may
// act on them. Every claim below is checkable and dated:
//   - line item "01_019_0120_1_1" for yard and garden — from the NDIS
//     Pricing Arrangements, which are REISSUED ANNUALLY
//   - NDIA contact 1800 800 110 — verify current
//   - short-notice cancellation rules — among the most frequently revised
//     parts of the pricing arrangements
//   - consumables generally not claimable — broadly right, confirm phrasing
//   - shared-household funding — an eligibility judgement, keep it hedged
//
// Recommended before launch: a read by someone who works with plans daily
// (a plan manager or coordinator, not just a proofread); a visible "last
// reviewed" date on this block; a short "this is general information, check
// with your plan manager" line; and a diarised re-check when the next
// pricing arrangements publish.
//
// The answers already hedge well ("often yes, but usually only", "not until
// it's funded, but you can ask for it"). Don't let a review flatten that
// into vagueness — the honesty is what earns a coordinator's trust.
const nonBrandedFaqItems = [
  {
    question: 'Can I still get cleaning funded if I live with family or housemates?',
    answer:
      'Often yes, but usually only for the spaces you\'re responsible for. The NDIS won\'t fund supports that duplicate what family or other household members already provide, and the NDIA considers whether a support is shared. In a share house, that can mean funding covers your own room and the parts of the home your disability stops you managing, rather than the whole house.',
  },
  {
    question: "Cleaning isn't in my plan. Can I still get it?",
    answer:
      "Not until it's funded, but you can ask for it. Core Supports funding is usually flexible, so if you already have Assistance with Daily Life funding you may be able to use some of it for cleaning. If you don't, raise it at your next plan check-in or ask the NDIA for a plan variation on 1800 800 110. An occupational therapist report describing what you can't safely do strengthens the case.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What will the cleaner actually do?',
    answer:
      "Everyday household cleaning you can't manage safely yourself: vacuuming, mopping, bathrooms, kitchen, dusting, laundry, changing linen and taking out bins. It's an hourly support, so you set the priorities for the time booked, and a two-hour visit won't cover a whole-house deep clean. Yard and garden work is a separate NDIS line item (01_019_0120_1_1), billed separately at its own rate.",
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I need to provide cleaning products and a vacuum?',
    answer:
      "That's set by your provider and recorded in your service agreement, so ask before the first visit. The NDIS funds the hour of support work, not consumables: cleaning products, mops and vacuums generally can't be claimed from your plan unless they're specialised items linked to your disability. If you'd rather your own products were used for allergy or sensory reasons, say so upfront.",
  },
  {
    question: 'What happens if I have to cancel a clean?',
    answer:
      "Your service agreement sets the notice period, so check it before you sign. If you cancel inside that window, a provider may be able to claim the session from your plan, but only if the agreement says so and they couldn't give the worker other paid work instead. Give as much notice as you can and you won't be charged.",
  },
];

// §16 Closing CTA — ⚠️ not in the brief, which has no closing-CTA row (and
// two mid-page rows reading "CTA -" with nothing after them).
//
// Uses "Get a fast quote" from the brief's own meta description and branded
// FAQ 5, rather than the sitewide "Get an instant quote" — *fast* is gentler
// than *instant* for this audience, and the brief chose it twice.
//
// ⚠️ Consider a second, coordinator-specific route here, tied to card 3's
// unresolved destination. Participants and coordinators want different next
// steps, and this page is the one place both are reading.
const closingCta = {
  heading: 'Tell us what would help.',
  lead: "A simple chat about what you need, when, and whether we're the right fit. No pressure, and we're happy to speak with your family, coordinator or plan manager too.",
  // ✅ Was "Get a fast quote", against the hero's "Get an instant quote". One
  // primary CTA per page, repeated, per BFD §9.5. See §1.
  cta: { label: 'Enquire today', href: quoteCta.href },
};
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={hero.kicker}
      headingLines={hero.headingLines}
      subheading={hero.subheading}
      lead={hero.lead}
      cta={hero.cta}
      image={hero.image}
    />

    {/* Trust bar + definition block share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim,
        so it is laid out across both — see the homepage for why a
        per-section background cannot do this. 75% keeps Dark Teal body
        copy above 4.5:1 over the darkest part of the frame. */}
    <Backdrop image={definitionBgImg} tint="muted" opacity={75}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock theme="muted" align="left" body={definitionBody} />
    </Backdrop>

    <PathwayCards heading={pathwayCards.heading} headingAccent={pathwayCards.headingAccent} cards={pathwayCards.cards} />

    <Callout tone="note" heading={worthKnowing.heading} body={worthKnowing.body} />

    {/* §7 credibility signals, §8 hero video and §9 "the why" are all
        EMPTY in the brief — boilerplate labels with no direction at all.
        Not built and not guessed: the deep-cleaning page's credibility
        block was filled on an assumption and removed again at TLB's
        direction, which is the precedent. They need a brief first. */}

    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading="NDIS house cleaning for people across the Northern Rivers and the Tweed"
      body={whoThisIsForBody}
    />

    {/* ✅ THE FULL FOOTPRINT, per the copy-rewrite brief §17, which specifies
        this list town for town. Replaces `nswLocationGroups`, which rendered
        six coastal Northern Rivers towns and no Tweed at all.

        The intro heading directly above promises the Tweed and this band
        dropped it, against BFD §2.1. On THIS page the inland towns matter more
        than on any other: Casino, Kyogle, Coraki and Woodburn are where a
        participant has the fewest local options and the most reason to be
        checking whether anyone covers them. Naming six coastal towns quietly
        tells everyone else they are not the market, which is the exact error
        §2.1 warns against. The header menu on this page already carried the
        wider list, so this was a template inconsistency rather than a writing
        problem.

        ⚠️ This widens the page across the Queensland border, so `areaServed`
        in the structured data above was widened with it. See the note there —
        if TLB does not take NDIS work in the Southern Gold Coast, both revert
        together. */}
    <TagCloud
      heading="Where we clean"
      subheading={footprintSubheading}
      groups={completeFootprintLocationGroups}
      note={["If a town isn't listed, ask us anyway."]}
    />

    <ContentGrid columns={2} heading="Why participants and coordinators choose us" blocks={whyChooseBlocks} />

    {/* 🔴 CONDITIONAL SECTION — see the `windowCleaningBody` note above before
        launching. It targets 400/mo that this page captured at zero, and it
        must not go live until somebody confirms TLB cleans windows for NDIS
        participants. Delete this block and its constant if the answer is no.

        `light`, not `muted`: the ContentGrid above and the TagCloud below both
        paint their own fills, so this keeps the page alternating. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="light"
      align="left"
      heading="NDIS window cleaning, and the jobs you can't safely reach"
      body={windowCleaningBody}
    />

    <TestimonialCarousel heading="What our clients say" testimonials={testimonials} uniform />

    {/* §13 "How we compare" is NOT built. The brief said only "[Insert
        comparison table here, if applicable]" — and "if applicable" suggests
        the author was unsure too. The shared table in src/data/comparison.ts
        is approved copy but wrong for this audience: "ongoing contracts with
        the region's leading real estate agencies" answers a household's
        question, not a participant's. A coordinator wants screening,
        insurance, plan types accepted, worker consistency and cancellation
        terms. Write NDIS-specific rows or leave it out — an off-target
        table costs more credibility than it earns. */}

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `instant` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={theLineHeading.instant}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.regular]}
    />

    {/* ✅ BOTH FAQ HEADINGS RENAMED (copy-rewrite brief §19 and §20).

        The page carries two FAQ blocks and the generic "Frequently Asked
        Questions" on the first made the split look like a build error. The
        first block is about working with TLB, the second is about the scheme.

        The second heading also lands `ndis house cleaning` contiguously, which
        is part of the H2 keyword component, and drops the Title Case that BFD
        §9.2 rules out. */}
    <Faq heading="Working with us" items={brandedFaqItems} />
    <Faq heading="NDIS house cleaning, common questions" items={nonBrandedFaqItems} />

    <CallToAction variant="secondary" heading={closingCta.heading} lead={closingCta.lead} cta={closingCta.cta} />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
